# 17. Known Limitation: Visual Model Latency

The largest current limitation is the local vision-language model.

For the supplied YouTube Short:

```text
Video duration:       approximately 63.88 s
Original frames:      1597
Sampled frames:       128
Candidate frames:     14
```

Sampling occurs every 0.5 seconds.

OpenCV filtering reduces the 128 sampled frames to 14 candidate frames before calling the VLM.

This significantly reduces unnecessary model calls.

However, `qwen3-vl:4b` is still slow on my local CPU.

A single candidate image can take several minutes to process.

I also tested sending all 14 candidate images in one request, but the request exceeded the model context limit.

Possible production improvements include:

* GPU inference
* smaller/faster VLM
* better candidate ranking
* lower-resolution inputs
* controlled image batching
* asynchronous workers

---

# 18. Reproducing the Tested Visual Example

The visual stage was verified using a real candidate frame from the supplied YouTube Short.

One known candidate was:

```text
frame_10.0.jpg
```

This frame contains the Avis Vascular Center promotional panel.

The same `classify_frame()` function used by the normal pipeline correctly identified the advertisement and returned visual evidence such as:

* Avis Vascular Center
* ₹4500
* phone number
* promotional Telugu text

The full candidate loop calls this same function for each selected candidate.

Because local CPU inference is slow, completing every candidate can require significant processing time.

---

# 19. Cost

The project currently uses local models rather than hosted model APIs.

Therefore:

```text
estimated hosted API cost = $0
```

This excludes:

* local electricity
* hardware cost
* CPU/GPU compute time

The current implementation trades hosted API cost for local inference latency.

---

# 20. Clean Run Note

The individual pipeline stages and API were tested during development.

However, because of the local VLM latency, I did not complete a final clean-checkout, full five-video end-to-end evaluation before submission.

A reviewer running the project on faster inference hardware should expect the vision stage to be more practical, but this submission does not claim that the entire five-video pipeline has already been fully validated on another environment.

